WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.500
Richard Feynman worked on the Manhattan&nbsp;
Project that built the atomic bomb.

00:00:04.500 --> 00:00:08.880
He uncovered what caused the space shuttle&nbsp;
Challenger disaster when no one else could.

00:00:08.880 --> 00:00:11.460
He won a Nobel Prize in Physics.

00:00:11.460 --> 00:00:15.840
He accomplished all this with an IQ of 125.

00:00:15.840 --> 00:00:19.860
You might think that’s a pretty high&nbsp;
IQ, considering the standard is 100.

00:00:19.860 --> 00:00:22.860
However, 1 in 20 people has that IQ.

00:00:22.860 --> 00:00:27.780
And it isn’t high enough to make it into&nbsp;
Mensa which accepts only the top 2 percent,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.780 --> 00:00:30.720
usually a score of 132 or higher.

00:00:30.720 --> 00:00:35.160
I was an ordinary person who studied&nbsp;
hard. There’s no miracle people.
&nbsp;

00:00:35.160 --> 00:00:39.300
He didn’t see himself as anything&nbsp;
special, as he said in this BBC interview.

00:00:39.300 --> 00:00:42.780
But what set him apart was his curiosity.

00:00:44.220 --> 00:00:47.280
Growing up in Far Rockaway,&nbsp;
a neighborhood in Queen’s,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.280 --> 00:00:50.520
New York, his dad encouraged&nbsp;
him to learn how things worked.

00:00:50.520 --> 00:00:53.100
Young Richard had a knack for fixing radios.

00:00:53.100 --> 00:00:58.140
His father expected him to become a scientist.&nbsp;
His sister Joan followed the same path.

00:00:58.140 --> 00:01:03.360
Richard gained his sense of humor from his&nbsp;
mum. He was a real character, and it showed.

00:01:03.360 --> 00:01:09.300
Now I’m going to discuss how we’ll look for&nbsp;
a new law. In general, we look for a new law&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.300 --> 00:01:15.840
by the following process. First, we guess it.&nbsp;
(Laughter) Well, don’t laugh, that’s really true.

00:01:15.840 --> 00:01:19.560
Feynman seriously considered&nbsp;
becoming a mathematician but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.560 --> 00:01:23.340
felt the discipline was too abstract,&nbsp;
and eventually switched to physics.

00:01:23.340 --> 00:01:28.320
He applied to Columbia University&nbsp;
but was rejected. Columbia had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.320 --> 00:01:32.460
a discriminatory policy limiting the&nbsp;
number of Jewish students it admitted.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.460 --> 00:01:37.740
He enrolled at MIT and graduated&nbsp;
with a Bachelor’s degree in 1939.

00:01:37.740 --> 00:01:43.380
He received a scholarship to Princeton for grad&nbsp;
studies on the condition that he remain unmarried.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.380 --> 00:01:49.140
The stipulation may have been intended to ensure&nbsp;
he fully focus on his studies. He did become&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.140 --> 00:01:54.000
engaged to his high school sweetheart, Arline&nbsp;
Greenbaum. But they didn’t have long together.

00:01:54.780 --> 00:02:00.420
She was diagnosed with tuberculosis, an&nbsp;
incurable disease at the time. They married&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.420 --> 00:02:06.600
on a ferry between New York and New Jersey in 1942&nbsp;
before Arline went straight to another hospital.

00:02:06.600 --> 00:02:10.260
She died three years later, exactly one month&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.260 --> 00:02:14.340
before the U.S. detonated the&nbsp;
world’s first nuclear weapon.

00:02:14.340 --> 00:02:18.660
The U.S. feared that Nazi Germany might&nbsp;
develop a nuclear weapon ahead of them,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.660 --> 00:02:22.080
so the Americans raced to&nbsp;
develop the atomic bomb first.

00:02:22.080 --> 00:02:25.800
The best and brightest scientists&nbsp;
signed up for the Manhattan Project,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.800 --> 00:02:29.760
where they worked out of a secret&nbsp;
research center in Los Alamos, New Mexico.

00:02:29.760 --> 00:02:34.380
Physicist Robert Wilson encouraged&nbsp;
Feynman to join the Manhattan Project,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.380 --> 00:02:39.420
as he had a reputation for being&nbsp;
exceptionally gifted at math and physics.

00:02:39.420 --> 00:02:42.960
He agreed and moved his sweetheart&nbsp;
to a hospital in Albuquerque,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.960 --> 00:02:45.720
New Mexico, where he could visit her on weekends.

00:02:45.720 --> 00:02:49.860
His work on the project included&nbsp;
calculating the energy released&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.860 --> 00:02:54.900
from the detonation of a bomb. He and&nbsp;
scientist Hans Bethe produced a formula&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.900 --> 00:02:58.320
used to determine the energy&nbsp;
yield of a nuclear explosive.

00:02:58.320 --> 00:03:03.660
Bethe used to poke fun at Feynman’s New York&nbsp;
accent, thinking it made him sound like a “bum”.

00:03:04.260 --> 00:03:10.140
During his downtime at Los Alamos, there wasn’t&nbsp;
much to amuse himself with, so Feynman fiddled&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.140 --> 00:03:15.840
around with picking locks and cracking safes that&nbsp;
contained the bomb project’s secret documents.

00:03:15.840 --> 00:03:19.320
The documents were originally&nbsp;
kept in filing cabinets with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.320 --> 00:03:24.060
padlocks which Feynman wrote in his&nbsp;
memoir “were as easy as pie to open.”

00:03:24.900 --> 00:03:28.020
Cabinets with combination&nbsp;
locks were later installed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.020 --> 00:03:30.120
but he figured out how to crack those, too.

00:03:30.120 --> 00:03:34.740
Once, when he visited the plant at Oak&nbsp;
Ridge, Tennessee, that processed uranium&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.740 --> 00:03:38.820
ore to be used in the bomb, managers&nbsp;
gathered to discuss a secret report.

00:03:38.820 --> 00:03:41.880
But the executive who had the&nbsp;
document stored in his safe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.880 --> 00:03:46.560
couldn’t access it because he didn’t know&nbsp;
the combination and his secretary was away.

00:03:46.560 --> 00:03:51.360
Feynman fiddled around with the safe and&nbsp;
“In 10 minutes, I had opened the safe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.360 --> 00:03:56.340
that contains all the secret documents&nbsp;
about the plant. They were astonished.”

00:03:56.340 --> 00:04:00.960
The Manhattan Project left a lasting mark&nbsp;
on Feynman’s legacy, even though he only&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.960 --> 00:04:06.480
played a junior role because he had a hand in&nbsp;
producing the deadliest weapon known to humankind.

00:04:06.480 --> 00:04:10.080
The head of the Manhattan&nbsp;
Project, Robert Oppenheimer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.080 --> 00:04:14.880
was also aggrieved by what scientists&nbsp;
had unleashed on the world. I told of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.880 --> 00:04:18.720
his despair and downfall in another video&nbsp;
which I’ve linked in my description.

00:04:18.720 --> 00:04:24.660
Feynman watched the detonation from a truck&nbsp;
parked 20 miles away. He claimed to be the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.660 --> 00:04:30.420
only one to watch the Trinity test without&nbsp;
wearing glasses or goggles. He thought the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.420 --> 00:04:36.000
truck’s windshield would protect him from&nbsp;
the harmful ultraviolet radiation, but,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.000 --> 00:04:41.280
at the last moment, he quickly dove onto the&nbsp;
floor as the sky lit up with a tremendous flash.

00:04:43.380 --> 00:04:47.520
After leaving the Manhattan Project,&nbsp;
he was no longer exempt from the draft,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.520 --> 00:04:53.280
but he avoided compulsory military service&nbsp;
when he was judged to be mentally incompetent!

00:04:53.820 --> 00:04:57.180
An army psychiatrist who assessed&nbsp;
Feynman wrote these words:

00:04:57.180 --> 00:05:00.900
Thinks people talk about him.
Thinks people stare at him.&nbsp;

00:05:00.900 --> 00:05:03.660
Auditory hypnogogic hallucinations.&nbsp;

00:05:03.660 --> 00:05:06.840
Talks to self.
Talks to deceased wife.&nbsp;

00:05:06.840 --> 00:05:11.700
Maternal aunt in mental institution.
Very peculiar stare.

00:05:12.360 --> 00:05:15.300
Feynman said most people laughed&nbsp;
when he told them the story.

00:05:15.300 --> 00:05:19.440
But he wasn’t very amused and said&nbsp;
it was all just a misunderstanding.

00:05:19.440 --> 00:05:26.280
Instead of enlisting, Feynman accepted a teaching&nbsp;
position at Cornell University for $4,000 a year.

00:05:26.280 --> 00:05:31.860
He wasn’t particularly motivated there and was&nbsp;
surprised when he got offers to teach at UCLA,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.860 --> 00:05:36.600
UC Berkeley, and the renowned Institute for&nbsp;
Advanced Study in Princeton, New Jersey.

00:05:36.600 --> 00:05:43.440
But he remained at Cornell and decided to develop&nbsp;
a new attitude. Why not just enjoy physics?

00:05:43.440 --> 00:05:46.920
And that is when his curiosity&nbsp;
about the world kicked in.

00:05:46.920 --> 00:05:50.160
One day, when he was eating&nbsp;
in the cafeteria at Cornell,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.160 --> 00:05:52.800
he noticed a student tossing a plate in the air.

00:05:53.520 --> 00:05:56.520
He noticed that the plate’s motion was similar to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.520 --> 00:05:59.220
how tiny particles move and&nbsp;
interact with each other.

00:05:59.220 --> 00:06:02.520
In the way that the plate&nbsp;
was affected by gravity and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.520 --> 00:06:06.720
air resistance, tiny particles were&nbsp;
also affected by different forces.

00:06:07.320 --> 00:06:12.720
This helped him better understand quantum&nbsp;
electrodynamics, or QED, a theory that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.720 --> 00:06:17.100
explains how atoms and light interact with&nbsp;
each other through the electromagnetic force.

00:06:17.100 --> 00:06:21.840
Imagine a baseball game where the&nbsp;
baseball represents a subatomic particle,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.840 --> 00:06:25.380
and the players represent the&nbsp;
force that acts on the ball.

00:06:25.380 --> 00:06:29.760
Feynman produced mathematical equations&nbsp;
that are like the rules of the game.

00:06:29.760 --> 00:06:32.760
They explain how the baseball, or particles,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.760 --> 00:06:37.140
are affected by the players, or force,&nbsp;
and how they interact with each other.

00:06:37.140 --> 00:06:40.440
He created diagrams that were very helpful for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.440 --> 00:06:43.860
scientists in understanding and&nbsp;
calculating these interactions.

00:06:43.860 --> 00:06:49.020
His curious nature allowed him to draw&nbsp;
connections between seemingly unrelated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:49.020 --> 00:06:52.500
things, between a plate and subatomic particles.

00:06:52.500 --> 00:06:59.280
For his work in quantum electrodynamics,&nbsp;
he won the Nobel Prize in Physics in 1965.

00:06:59.280 --> 00:07:01.800
He shared the prize with two other scientists&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:01.800 --> 00:07:04.980
who found their own methods for&nbsp;
the same problem independently.

00:07:04.980 --> 00:07:10.260
Many believe he could have won the Nobel&nbsp;
Prize again when he and physicist Murray&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:10.260 --> 00:07:13.500
Gell-Mann came up with a theory&nbsp;
that explained the weak force&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.500 --> 00:07:17.940
which makes certain atoms change and&nbsp;
give off energy. They found that tiny&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:17.940 --> 00:07:22.380
particles called neutrinos carry the&nbsp;
weak force and make the atoms change.

00:07:22.380 --> 00:07:27.600
It’s like a game of pool, where the neutrinos&nbsp;
are the cue ball that hits the other balls,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:27.600 --> 00:07:30.120
or atoms, and makes them move. Weak Force Theory

00:07:30.120 --> 00:07:32.520
He never really put down roots at Cornell.

00:07:32.520 --> 00:07:38.580
He moved from student residences to guest houses&nbsp;
to squatting with friends. He reportedly liked&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:38.580 --> 00:07:42.960
to date undergrads, hire prostitutes,&nbsp;
and sleep with the wives of friends.

00:07:42.960 --> 00:07:48.000
He also didn’t appreciate the weather in&nbsp;
upstate New York and decided to trade the cold,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.000 --> 00:07:53.220
slushy winters for sunny weather by moving&nbsp;
to the west coast to teach physics at the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:53.220 --> 00:07:57.900
California Institute of Technology in&nbsp;
1950, where he remained until his death.

00:07:58.980 --> 00:08:03.000
Before heading to Caltech, he spent&nbsp;
a year on sabbatical in Brazil,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:03.000 --> 00:08:06.360
where he taught physics at a&nbsp;
research center in Rio de Janeiro.

00:08:06.360 --> 00:08:07.980
He learned to speak Portuguese&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:10.020 --> 00:08:14.520
and played the Brazilian instrument&nbsp;
frigideira which is based on a frying pan. SU

00:08:14.520 --> 00:08:18.660
He loved playing instruments and also&nbsp;
took up the Bongo and conga drums.

00:08:18.660 --> 00:08:22.680
Upon returning to America, he&nbsp;
devoted himself to revamping&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:22.680 --> 00:08:25.080
Caltech’s physics curriculum for freshmen.

00:08:25.080 --> 00:08:30.480
He and two other professors transcribed their&nbsp;
lectures and turned them into a set of textbooks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:30.480 --> 00:08:33.600
that went on to sell more than a million copies&nbsp;
in English. (The Feynman Lectures on Physics)

00:08:33.600 --> 00:08:38.760
During his early years at Caltech, he&nbsp;
also provided a mathematical theory that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:38.760 --> 00:08:43.320
explains the strange behavior of liquid&nbsp;
helium at extremely low temperatures.

00:08:43.320 --> 00:08:51.000
When liquid helium comes close to absolute zero,&nbsp;
or minus 273 degrees Celsius, it turns into a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:51.000 --> 00:08:54.720
superfluid that does the impossible, leaking&nbsp;
through this container! -273.15 degrees Celsius

00:08:54.720 --> 00:08:59.280
He also studied how electrons behave&nbsp;
when they collide at very high speed.

00:08:59.280 --> 00:09:04.440
He found they act like marbles colliding, in&nbsp;
which they can change direction and velocity.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:04.440 --> 00:09:09.120
This helped scientists make new discoveries&nbsp;
in the field of high-energy physics.

00:09:09.720 --> 00:09:12.840
His work was like a brushstroke on canvas,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:12.840 --> 00:09:16.080
creating a masterpiece of&nbsp;
scientific understanding.

00:09:16.080 --> 00:09:20.040
He also loved art, and took&nbsp;
courses in painting and drawing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:20.040 --> 00:09:22.560
and became really good at portrait sketching.

00:09:22.560 --> 00:09:26.940
He signed his works with the pen name&nbsp;
Ofey as he wanted the art to speak for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:26.940 --> 00:09:30.720
itself rather than have people think&nbsp;
it was the work of a famous physicist.

00:09:31.740 --> 00:09:34.260
He wasn’t only interested in how things looked&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:34.260 --> 00:09:37.740
on the outside but the beauty&nbsp;
that lay beneath the surface.

00:09:37.740 --> 00:09:42.180
This is the incredible way he&nbsp;
described a flower to the BBC.

00:09:42.180 --> 00:09:48.720
I have a friend who’s an artist…He’ll hold up a&nbsp;
flower and say “look how beautiful it is,” and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:48.720 --> 00:09:54.060
I’ll agree. Then he says “I as an artist&nbsp;
can see how beautiful this is but you as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:54.060 --> 00:10:00.120
a scientist take this all apart and it becomes a&nbsp;
dull thing,” and I think that he’s kind of nutty.

00:10:00.120 --> 00:10:05.520
I see much more about the flower than he sees. I&nbsp;
could imagine the cells in there, the complicated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:05.520 --> 00:10:12.780
actions inside, which also have a beauty.The fact&nbsp;
that the colors in the flower evolved in order to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:12.780 --> 00:10:17.820
attract insects to pollinate it is interesting;&nbsp;
it means that insects can see the color.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:18.480 --> 00:10:25.380
It adds a question: does this aesthetic sense&nbsp;
also exist in the lower forms? Why is it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:25.380 --> 00:10:33.120
aesthetic? All kinds of interesting questions&nbsp;
which the science knowledge only adds to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:33.120 --> 00:10:38.700
excitement, the mystery and the awe of a flower.&nbsp;
It only adds. I don’t understand how it subtracts.

00:10:39.420 --> 00:10:42.480
He loved to ask questions&nbsp;
and think outside the box.

00:10:42.480 --> 00:10:46.740
That led him to get to the bottom of&nbsp;
what caused the Challenger explosion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:46.740 --> 00:10:50.220
in 1986 that killed the seven astronauts onboard.

00:10:50.760 --> 00:10:54.000
Feynman was already well-respected&nbsp;
in the scientific community,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:54.000 --> 00:10:58.860
but it was his investigation of Challenger&nbsp;
that made him famous to the general public.

00:10:58.860 --> 00:11:03.000
At first, he was hesitant to join the&nbsp;
Commission tasked with investigating&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:03.000 --> 00:11:06.480
the disaster because he preferred&nbsp;
to stay out of anything political,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:06.480 --> 00:11:11.640
but his third wife Gweneth reminded him&nbsp;
of his usefulness; because he didn’t do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:11.640 --> 00:11:16.440
things the conventional way, maybe he’d spot&nbsp;
something others couldn’t. She was right.

00:11:16.440 --> 00:11:20.160
He realized that NASA and its contractors tended&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:20.160 --> 00:11:24.540
to discourage their own employees from&nbsp;
criticizing legitimate safety issues.

00:11:24.540 --> 00:11:27.240
He saw the same type of thing in Los Alamos&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:27.240 --> 00:11:30.480
involving the security of&nbsp;
those top-secret documents.

00:11:30.480 --> 00:11:34.980
Engineers at the firm NASA contracted to&nbsp;
make the rocket boosters were concerned&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:34.980 --> 00:11:38.520
that the rubber gaskets that sealed the&nbsp;
boosters would fail in cold weather.

00:11:38.520 --> 00:11:42.720
However, there was pressure from&nbsp;
NASA, so the launch went ahead anyway.

00:11:42.720 --> 00:11:47.400
Feynman suspected that rubber&nbsp;
gaskets sealing the rocket boosters,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:47.400 --> 00:11:52.380
called O-Rings, couldn’t expand quickly&nbsp;
enough in cold weather to fully seal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:52.380 --> 00:11:57.060
the joints and failed to stop gas from&nbsp;
escaping, leading to the catastrophe.

00:11:57.060 --> 00:12:03.060
To test his theory, he dunked some rubber&nbsp;
O-rings into a glass of ice water on live TV.

00:12:12.300 --> 00:12:16.920
In other words, for a few seconds at&nbsp;
least, and more seconds than that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:16.920 --> 00:12:22.440
there’s no resilience in this particular material&nbsp;
when it’s at a temperature of 32 degrees.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:23.520 --> 00:12:27.660
I believe that has some&nbsp;
significance for our problem.

00:12:27.660 --> 00:12:31.380
By the time of the hearing,&nbsp;
cancer had weakened Feynman.

00:12:31.380 --> 00:12:36.300
A decade before, he was diagnosed with&nbsp;
abdominal cancer that formed a massive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:36.300 --> 00:12:40.320
tumor on his abdomen, which crushed&nbsp;
one of his kidneys and his spleen.

00:12:40.320 --> 00:12:43.860
Surgery removed the tumor,&nbsp;
but he never fully recovered.

00:12:43.860 --> 00:12:47.280
Near the end of his life, he tried to visit Tuva,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:47.280 --> 00:12:52.620
a remote region in southern Siberia bordering&nbsp;
Mongolia that was then part of the Soviet Union.

00:12:52.620 --> 00:12:55.800
He wanted to learn about a&nbsp;
place he knew nothing about.

00:12:55.800 --> 00:12:59.340
But bureaucratic red tape&nbsp;
prevented him from traveling there.

00:12:59.340 --> 00:13:01.740
He died before his visa arrived.

00:13:02.520 --> 00:13:09.660
Richard Feynman passed away on&nbsp;
February 15, 1988, at the age of 69.

00:13:10.200 --> 00:13:14.700
In 2009, his daughter Michelle made the&nbsp;
journey to Tuva to carry out his dream.

00:13:14.700 --> 00:13:18.780
Shortly before he died, The&nbsp;
Los Angeles Times interviewed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:18.780 --> 00:13:21.240
Richard and asked what he was most proud of.

00:13:21.240 --> 00:13:27.180
He replied: “That I was able to love my first&nbsp;
wife with as deep a love as I was able to.”

00:13:27.840 --> 00:13:32.400
After Arline died, he wrote her&nbsp;
letters expressing his heartbreak.

00:13:32.400 --> 00:13:37.980
I find it hard to understand in my mind what&nbsp;
it means to love you after you are dead—but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:37.980 --> 00:13:42.420
I still want to comfort and take care of you&nbsp;
– and I want you to love me and care for me.

00:13:43.020 --> 00:13:49.080
I loved you in so many ways so much. And&nbsp;
now it is clearly even more true — you can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:49.080 --> 00:13:55.020
give me nothing now yet I love you so that you&nbsp;
stand in my way of loving anyone else — but I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:55.020 --> 00:13:59.940
want you to stand there. You, dead, are&nbsp;
so much better than anyone else alive.

00:14:00.720 --> 00:14:05.460
P.S. Please excuse my not mailing this&nbsp;
— but I don’t know your new address.

00:14:07.200 --> 00:14:11.640
While Feynman may have believed that his love&nbsp;
for his wife was his greatest achievement,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:11.640 --> 00:14:16.440
to the world, his passion and&nbsp;
devotion to science was incomparable.

00:14:16.440 --> 00:14:19.380
Yet he insisted he was nothing special.

00:14:19.380 --> 00:14:26.580
There’s no talent, a special miracle ability to&nbsp;
understand quantum physics, or a miracle ability&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:27.480 --> 00:14:34.080
to imagine electromagnetic fields that comes&nbsp;
without practice, and reading, and learning,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:34.080 --> 00:14:38.880
and study. So if you say, you take an ordinary&nbsp;
person, who is willing to devote a great deal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:38.880 --> 00:14:44.160
of time and study and work and thinking and&nbsp;
mathematics, then he’s become a scientist.

00:14:44.160 --> 00:14:49.020
Who knows if he would have scored higher&nbsp;
had he taken another IQ test in school.

00:14:49.020 --> 00:14:53.340
Regardless, he was undoubtedly a genius thanks to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:53.340 --> 00:14:57.480
his perpetual curiosity to learn&nbsp;
why things work the way they do.

00:14:58.200 --> 00:15:02.700
If you're curious, like Feynman, and want to&nbsp;
learn about why things work the way they do,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:02.700 --> 00:15:06.960
Brilliant.org is a FREE website&nbsp;
and app where you can learn math,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:06.960 --> 00:15:09.360
science, and computer science interactively.

00:15:09.360 --> 00:15:14.160
My viewers especially love their Scientific&nbsp;
Thinking course, where instead of learning&nbsp;&nbsp;

